  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x^2 + z^2)/2, drawn at 0.5 × loss over x and z from -2 to 2. x is the steep direction. With no noise, x overshoots above learning rate 0.4 and grows without limit above 0.8.",
    "Each step moves by -learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise has standard deviation 1.5·sqrt((64−B)/(63·B)) per setting for batch B, centred on zero and exactly 0 at B = 64.",
    "Both arrows are drawn at 2 × their true step length in bowl units. Orange is the mini-batch step the ball takes next. Grey is the step the full-data slope would give. Each step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "The ball makes 5 updates per second at 1× speed, and Take one step makes exactly one. A run ends at 40 steps (finished). It ends early if the loss goes above 8 (diverged).",
    "The noise sequence comes from a fixed seed and continues across new runs, so Reset replays it from the start. A ball that leaves the bowl is drawn at its edge, and the loss readout keeps its true value."
  ]
}